00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.520
After spending 7 days in orbit around the&nbsp;
earth, the Space Shuttle Orbiter now has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:16.760
arguably the most difficult portion of&nbsp;
its mission to complete. A hell blazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:21.960
journey through the earth’s upper atmosphere.&nbsp;
Where it will travel so fast that it will rip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:27.360
air molecules apart, forming a layer of&nbsp;
superheated plasma around the aircraft.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.560
Re-Entry is where the Space Shuttle&nbsp;
truly became a one of a kind spacecraft.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.840
The Space Shuttle was a radical&nbsp;
new idea. A spacecraft capable of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.120
not only surviving the immense heat of&nbsp;
re-entry, but capable of transitioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.200
to aerodynamic flight, which required&nbsp;
careful moulding of its wings and tail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.000
balancing the needs of unpowered glider&nbsp;
with the needs of a re-entry vehicle

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.560
This is the Insane Engineering of the Re-Entry

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:02.200
The Re-entry procedure begins with a 2-4 minute&nbsp;
burn of the orbital manoeuvring system engines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.640
while the space shuttle is upside&nbsp;
down and travelling backwards.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.520
With an orbital speed of around 7 kilometres&nbsp;
per second, the OMS pods need to reduce the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:17.320
orbiter's speed by just 0.1 kilometre per&nbsp;
second. 1.3% of its velocity, to lower its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:22.040
orbit enough to bring it into a collision&nbsp;
course with the earth’s upper atmosphere.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:26.400
A precise manoeuvre; bleed too little&nbsp;
speed and the orbiter will overshoot its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.440
narrow window for success, skimming through&nbsp;
the thin upper atmosphere and potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.920
bouncing back into space. The orbiter&nbsp;
has wings that generate lift, after all.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:42.160
Bleed too much speed and the orbiter will&nbsp;
descend through the atmosphere too fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.640
reaching the thick lower atmosphere&nbsp;
before enough speed has been leached away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:53.280
resulting in catastrophic overheating. This narrow&nbsp;
entry window was called the entry flight corridor.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.960
Once the delicate retrofiring sequence has&nbsp;
been completed. The next phase of re-entry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:03.000
begins. The reaction control system flips&nbsp;
the shuttle around and places it into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.080
40 degree upwards pitch angle, ready&nbsp;
to meet the earth’s atmosphere. [REF]

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:12.480
Entering the upper atmosphere at 30 times the&nbsp;
speed of sound. The speed is so great that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:18.360
begins to rip air molecules apart, creating a&nbsp;
glowing cloud of charged plasma around the lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:24.720
surface of the orbiter. With peak temperatures&nbsp;
reaching 1650 degree celsius (3000 F).

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.480
Nothing like this had ever been attempted.&nbsp;
A blunt body capsule, like every other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:35.440
re-entry capsule to date, designed purely for&nbsp;
thermal protection is an engineering challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:40.160
but tack on the needs of an aircraft&nbsp;
and the task gets vastly more difficult.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:46.160
Thankfully NASA had a test run in 1959&nbsp;
with the X-15. The fastest plane in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.120
history of humankind, and it advanced&nbsp;
our understanding of hypersonic flight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.720
providing many lessons that were&nbsp;
incorporated into the space shuttles design.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:01.640
However the X-15 had one major advantage over the&nbsp;
Space Shuttle. It didn’t need to launch itself to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:07.880
orbit. It didn’t even launch from the ground.&nbsp;
Instead launching from the belly of a B-52.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:13.440
This allowed the X-15 to use a state of the&nbsp;
art advanced heat resistant aerospace metal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.240
inconel X, with a max operating temperature of 980&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.400
degrees celsius (1800 F). The Space&nbsp;
Shuttle could not use this metal.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:26.960
Inconel X is too heavy, about 180%&nbsp;
heavier than an equivalent aluminium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.400
airframe. A massive issue for an aircraft&nbsp;
designed to be carried to orbit. [REF]

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.840
The Space Shuttle’s airframe therefore is not&nbsp;
made from inconel X. It is composed of lightweight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:46.720
aluminium, which has a max operating temperature&nbsp;
of just 177 degrees. 5 times lower than Inconel X.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:49.640
The orbiter would experience temperatures 10 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.840
greater than this for extended&nbsp;
periods of its re-entry flight.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.320
To make matters worse, one of the&nbsp;
principle lessons learned during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:03.040
the X-15 program was that the hot pink foam&nbsp;
ablative coating sprayed onto the plane for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.240
top speed flights was completely&nbsp;
unsuitable for the space shuttle.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:11.800
An ablative coating is a sacrificial&nbsp;
material designed to gradually burn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:15.440
and fall away from the aircraft,&nbsp;
pulling the heat away with it.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.720
However, the ablative coating of the X-15 had a&nbsp;
nasty habit of burning away from the nose of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:26.520
plane, and begin attaching itself to the cockpit&nbsp;
windows, rendering the pilots completely blind.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:32.440
Which presented a bit of a problem. At one&nbsp;
point the engineers of the X-15 considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:38.080
attaching a small explosive to the window, and&nbsp;
intentionally exploding the outer pane of glass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:42.800
to remove the ablative stained window,&nbsp;
leaving only the inner pane for landing.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.400
Thankfully they came up with the much less&nbsp;
risky solution of installing a mechanical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:51.680
eyelid to the left window that remained&nbsp;
closed until the high speed portion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:57.160
the flight concluded. Providing the pilot&nbsp;
one clean window to land with. An extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:02.480
primitive solution that created stability&nbsp;
issues as the open eyelid acted like a canard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:06.160
causing the plane to pitch&nbsp;
up, roll right and yaw right.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:10.720
Even more terrifyingly, this coating&nbsp;
became explosive when mixed with liquid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.600
oxygen and could be triggered by even a&nbsp;
slight impact. A massive safety concern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:21.080
for a plane that required a liquid oxygen&nbsp;
oxidizer to operate. A danger that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:27.440
be multiplied many fold with a vehicle filled&nbsp;
with half a million litres of liquid oxygen.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:32.200
The ablative was also not reusable, which&nbsp;
would drastically increase the cost of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:37.400
refurbishing the shuttle between flights.The&nbsp;
space shuttle would need to be better.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.480
As the space shuttle descended&nbsp;
through the atmosphere its nose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:43.400
and wings bore the brunt of the re-entry heat.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:48.720
The high pressure shock waves forming around&nbsp;
them have created a layer of superheated plasma,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:53.640
if this heat managed to find its way into the&nbsp;
delicate aluminium framework inside the orbiter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:57.800
it would be game over. This is exactly&nbsp;
what happened to the Columbia shuttle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:01.080
as a result of damage to the&nbsp;
leading edge of these wings.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:04.280
The first step to protect the&nbsp;
orbiter's surface was to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:09.280
this super heated plasma as far away&nbsp;
from the surface as possible. The nose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:14.840
wings and belly of the orbiter was carefully&nbsp;
crafted to ensure shockwaves were kept at bay.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:19.480
We can see how using schlieren imaging.&nbsp;
A pointed missile-like vehicle pierces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:23.880
through the air with efficiency and&nbsp;
in doing so creates a shock wave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:28.280
attached to its nose at an angle&nbsp;
determined by its mach number.

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:32.000
This shockwave is simply a region&nbsp;
of incredibly high pressure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:35.680
and with that pressure comes&nbsp;
incredibly high temperatures

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:42.800
This can have disastrous effects, as DARPA&nbsp;
experienced twice in 2010 and 2011 when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:48.040
testing their sharp nosed hypersonic&nbsp;
reentry vehicle. The HTV-2. Within 9&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:52.520
minutes of re-entering earth's atmosphere,&nbsp;
both vehicles disintegrated as a result of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:58.560
the 1930 degree heat penetrating their&nbsp;
metallic skin. The HTV-2 completed its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.800
mission of collecting hypersonic flight&nbsp;
data, but the space shuttle needed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:08.000
not only survive this mode of flight, but to&nbsp;
do it repeatedly with minimal refurbishment.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.160
A good portion of this heat could be&nbsp;
kept away from the aircraft skin with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:17.400
blunt body design.Which creates a rounded&nbsp;
bow shock wave with a layer of insulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:22.480
lower pressure air between the vehicle and&nbsp;
the shockwave. Reducing heat transfer rates.

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:26.920
For this reason the Space Shuttles surface is&nbsp;
carefully moulded to take advantage of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:32.560
phenomenon. The rounded nose cross section&nbsp;
gradually transitions to a blunted triangle.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:37.160
This shape minimised the heat reaching the&nbsp;
less protected side wall of the shuttle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:40.160
which used lower temperature insulation.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:45.840
We are now 7 minutes into the re-entry procedure,&nbsp;
having descended 50 kilometres in altitude,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:51.680
but only shedding 0.5 kilometres per second&nbsp;
off our velocity, we have entered the period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:56.840
of maximum reentry heat. A confluence&nbsp;
of speed and atmospheric density.

00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:01.360
The layer of superheated plasma surrounding&nbsp;
the shuttle is blocking communication to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.760
computers and astronauts inside. A result&nbsp;
of free electrons in the plasma interfering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:12.360
with electromagnetic communication techniques. A&nbsp;
problem that would last for the next 12 minutes.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:17.640
For now the shuttle is operating on its own&nbsp;
telemetry data. Ensuring that a 40 degree angle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:24.440
of attack is maintained. Managing that angle with&nbsp;
a velocity of 6.5 kilometres per second was no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:31.120
easy task. A massive control surface was needed.&nbsp;
The elevons on the outer wing would not suffice.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:36.040
This is where the rear body flap came into&nbsp;
play. It was a massive control surface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:40.400
underneath the shuttle's main engines,&nbsp;
covered in high temperature insulation.

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:43.360
The body flap doubled as a&nbsp;
heat shield for the shuttles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:47.440
main engines. With no cooling liquid&nbsp;
hydrogen running through the nozzle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:51.320
the flap was needed to shield the&nbsp;
engines from the heat of re-entry.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:57.000
On the Space Shuttle’s third flight the Kuiper&nbsp;
Airborne Observatory flew underneath the orbiter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:03.400
as it re-entered and captured an infrared image of&nbsp;
its searing hot belly. An experimental program to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:09.040
validate NASA’s newly developed computational&nbsp;
calculations and experimental testing.

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:15.720
This is what it saw [REF] The nose and leading&nbsp;
edges are a scorching 1500 degrees celsius. Far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:20.080
beyond what the Aluminium airframe&nbsp;
underneath is capable of enduring.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:25.240
These leading edges, experiencing the hottest&nbsp;
temperatures, needed the most heat resistant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:31.080
material on the entire shuttle, a reinforced&nbsp;
carbon-carbon composite. One of the amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:36.360
materials created in the years between the&nbsp;
development of the X-15 and the Space Shuttle.

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:41.000
A carbon composite manufactured&nbsp;
with a special post processing step.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:45.680
It was initially manufactured like any&nbsp;
other carbon fibre part. A carbon fibre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:49.640
weave moulded into shape and bound&nbsp;
together with a resin. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:52.720
the heat of re-entry would&nbsp;
set the hydrocarbon resin on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:58.080
fire without special treatment. The post&nbsp;
processing step would solve this problem.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.240
The carbon composite is placed in a&nbsp;
vacuum chamber and heated, causing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:07.800
the hydrocarbon resin to decompose, releasing&nbsp;
the hydrogens, leaving layers of pure carbon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:13.920
behind. Graphite. Carbon fibres bound together&nbsp;
by a maze of graphite. Strong, and capable of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:22.040
withstanding 1510 degrees celsius. They face&nbsp;
head on into the inferno of hypersonic flight.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:27.120
The leading edge of the orbiter's wings&nbsp;
is composed of 22 of these carbon carbon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:31.440
panels. With sealing strips covering&nbsp;
expansion gaps between each panel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:36.760
an essential solution to a lesson hard&nbsp;
won during the development of the X-15.

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:41.240
To investigate the heat of hypersonic&nbsp;
flight the X-15 was painted with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:46.960
special kind of paint that reacts to heat,&nbsp;
and after one flight the X-15 returned with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:51.680
strange wedge shaped patterns emanating&nbsp;
from the leading edge expansion joints.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:56.640
Small gaps in the leading edge to allow the&nbsp;
inconel skin to bend and contort with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:02.040
wing without buckling. This localised heating&nbsp;
was happening as a result of turbulent flow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:06.840
increasing the rate of heat transfer to&nbsp;
the metallic skin of the aircraft. To fix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:11.800
this small floating strips of inconel&nbsp;
were placed over the expansion gaps..

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:16.440
These strips of reinforced carbon&nbsp;
carbon serve the very same purpose.

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:19.240
However, there was one more problem. Carbon is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:23.400
thermally conductive material,&nbsp;
not ideal for a heat shield.

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:27.240
The space shuttle has been travelling&nbsp;
through the atmosphere for 15 minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:32.000
but is still travelling at 6 kilometres&nbsp;
per second and contact has not been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:36.080
reestablished through the cloud of&nbsp;
plasma surrounding the aircraft.

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:39.760
This is a sustained heat, and&nbsp;
with carbon being conductive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:44.880
this heat could make its way to the&nbsp;
aluminium airframe over this period of time.

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:49.920
If these carbon carbon shields were attached&nbsp;
directly to the airframe the heat would come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:55.240
into direct contact with the metal, raising&nbsp;
it above its max operating temperature.

00:11:55.240 --> 00:12:00.280
To prevent this the panels were fitted with&nbsp;
inconel attachment points that attached to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:05.240
aluminium airframe with inconel bolts.&nbsp;
Inconel, being mostly made of steel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:10.520
is a poor conductor of heat and could handle&nbsp;
the heat the carbon parts transferred to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:13.920
without easily transferring&nbsp;
that heat to the aluminium.

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:17.400
This wasn’t enough to protect the&nbsp;
interior of the shuttle however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:21.920
a layer of insulating tiles were placed&nbsp;
underneath the carbon carbon shield too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:26.480
The same insulation that was used on&nbsp;
the underside of the entire orbiter.

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:32.600
The Space Shuttle Columbia had 32000 of these&nbsp;
tiles, in two flavours, low temperature and high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:38.640
temperature, white and black. Both of these&nbsp;
tiles were made from the same base material.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:45.040
Silica fibres just a few millimetres thick,&nbsp;
which by volume made up just 10% of the tile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:50.200
the remaining 90% was nothing by air.&nbsp;
An excellent insulating material,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:55.680
a thick and light tile, composed of a&nbsp;
material with a high operating temperature.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:13:00.280
The coating is where the tiles differ. The black&nbsp;
tiles, installed on the lower portion of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:05.760
shuttle, were covered in a black borosilicate&nbsp;
and tetraboron silicide glass coating.

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:09.840
This black coating helps dissipate heat&nbsp;
before it can conduct into the vulnerable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:16.080
inner structure. Black for the same reason the&nbsp;
SR-71 is black. Kirchoff’s Rule of Radiation.

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:19.240
This rule tells us a good infrared heat absorber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:25.040
basically any black object, is also an equally&nbsp;
effective heat emitter. With the heat gained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:29.800
from hypersonic flight vastly higher than&nbsp;
the heat gained from solar radiation, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:36.160
preferable to prioritise outward heat radiation,&nbsp;
than minimising heat gained from solar radiation.

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:40.800
So why isn’t the entire space&nbsp;
shuttle black, like the SR-71?

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:47.840
The shuttle has one important difference with the&nbsp;
SR-71, it exited earth’s atmosphere. Once outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:53.240
the earth's atmosphere the intensity of solar&nbsp;
radiation increases drastically, with the longest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.240 --> 00:14:00.080
orbiter mission at 17 days, this heat had plenty&nbsp;
of time to conduct through to the people inside.

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:05.840
The orbiter did have huge radiator panels to&nbsp;
reject heat to space, but to minimise their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:11.920
size we want to reflect as much of that heat back&nbsp;
into space, and for that reason they are white.

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:14.480
These tiles were coated with&nbsp;
a similar glass coating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:18.840
but with an aluminium oxide additive&nbsp;
to provide the white colouring. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:22.880
coatings also helped to waterproof&nbsp;
and strengthen these porous tiles.

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:29.840
The tiles were assembled like a giant precisely&nbsp;
engineered puzzle with 32,000 pieces. Each tile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:35.080
assigned a serial number that corresponded&nbsp;
to their location, shape and thickness.

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:40.320
The tiles were created by mixing cotton like&nbsp;
silica fibres with water, this mixture was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:45.560
then cast into large blocks, and were then&nbsp;
dried in a microwave oven. Once dried they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:51.600
could be cut into smaller sheets. [Footage]
The tiles varied from 25 millimetres to 127&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:59.760
millimetres thick. With the 127 millimetres&nbsp;
tiles insulating up 1280 degrees celsius.

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:02.560
These sheets were then into their precise shapes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:08.040
according to their assigned serial numbers,&nbsp;
using a computer controlled milling machine.

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:11.960
We are now coming through the end of&nbsp;
the communication blackout, and enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:18.120
of the heat of re-entry has reached the aluminium&nbsp;
airframe to cause it to expand. A major problem as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:23.360
these tiles are not flexible in the slightest,&nbsp;
and are quite fragile. The expanding airframe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:28.600
could easily break the tiles and open up a path&nbsp;
for the superheated gas to penetrate inside.

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:31.680
The engineers needed a way&nbsp;
to allow the structure to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:35.480
move beneath the tiles without&nbsp;
causing the tiles to pop off.

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:40.960
To do this, the tiles were first glued in&nbsp;
groups to a layer of flexible nomex felt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:46.480
This nomex fabric allowed the structure beneath&nbsp;
to flex, expanding the lower side of the felt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:50.840
without transferring that strain to&nbsp;
the upper layer attached to the tiles.

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:55.400
These tiles and felt were then attached&nbsp;
to the shuttle using the same adhesive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.400 --> 00:16:01.680
A commercially available high temperature RTV&nbsp;
adhesive. A bright red silicone based adhesive.

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:04.040
To prevent collisions between tiles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:09.000
gaps between 0.64 millimetres and&nbsp;
1.9 millimetres were included.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:13.960
Thankfully the issue of turbulent flow created&nbsp;
by expansion gaps primarily affected the leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:18.600
edges of the wing, so these gaps were not&nbsp;
covered on the exterior of the shuttle.

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:23.880
In areas exposed to lesser temperatures flexible&nbsp;
heat shields were used. Constructed from high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:29.080
temperature silica and nomex fibres to create&nbsp;
a flexible fabric which were sewn together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:34.400
with the same fibres, giving a quilted blanket&nbsp;
appearance. With the nomex fabric capable of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:40.640
resisting temperatures up to 370 degrees and the&nbsp;
silica fabric capable of tolerating temperatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:46.480
up to 650 degrees. These were lighter and&nbsp;
easier to replace than the tiles. Being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:52.360
bonded directly to the structure once again&nbsp;
with that bright red silicone RTV adhesive.

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:58.640
We are now out of the communication black out&nbsp;
and approaching 4 km/s or mach 12 in velocity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.640 --> 00:17:04.280
and are about 45 kilometres in altitude. About&nbsp;
4 times the cruising altitude of an airliner.

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:08.280
The orbiter has maintained that 40&nbsp;
degree angle of attack this entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:12.400
time. [Page 239]. Being forced to hold an&nbsp;
angle of attack this large comes with some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:17.400
control problems. The orbiter needs to&nbsp;
land precisely on a landing strip from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:22.640
orbit. Managing its trajectory while being&nbsp;
stuck with its nose up would be difficult.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:27.880
Especially for a glider with no way to add&nbsp;
energy once its been bled away by drag.

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:30.360
If the Space Shuttle wanted&nbsp;
to lower its lift and lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:34.080
its altitude faster it could&nbsp;
not lower its angle of attack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:39.760
So the shuttle needed a special way to adjust&nbsp;
its trajectory to target its landing area.

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:43.880
Instead the orbiter decreased&nbsp;
its lift by banking. This split&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.880 --> 00:17:48.000
the lift into vertical and horizontal&nbsp;
components. Reducing the lift keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:52.480
the shuttle in the air and trading&nbsp;
it for lift that moved it sideways.

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:57.640
This is what a typical re-entry flight profile&nbsp;
looked like. This line is the angle of attack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.640 --> 00:18:01.480
keeping steady up until Mach 12&nbsp;
and then gradually reducing as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:05.320
descends through the thick lower&nbsp;
atmosphere. And this is the bank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:11.200
angle. As the orbiter crashes down from&nbsp;
orbit, it banks wildly from side to side.

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:16.280
The astronauts inside, who are already&nbsp;
pointed 40 degrees up, are not tilted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.280 --> 00:18:23.400
on their sides up 70 degrees. The orbiter is&nbsp;
essentially doing the fastest drift in history.

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:27.840
There are some quirks of flying this high&nbsp;
in the atmosphere at hypersonic speeds.

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:34.000
The bank angle controls actually work in reverse&nbsp;
to normal. If we deflect the right elevon down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:37.160
it should increase lift on the&nbsp;
right wing and cause that wing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:41.920
to rise, banking the aircraft&nbsp;
anti-clockwise, but it doesn’t.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:47.440
When the elevon is deflected downwards, into the&nbsp;
already compressed hypersonic air it causes drag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:52.880
to rise on the outer wing. This causes the&nbsp;
orbiter to turn its nose in that direction.

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:57.720
As the nose turns in the direction of the&nbsp;
deflected elevon, it shields that wing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.720 --> 00:19:02.640
and causes a decrease in lift, causing&nbsp;
the shuttle to bank in that direction.

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:08.240
So, where a downwards deflecting elevon would&nbsp;
typically cause an increase in lift on the wing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:14.040
here, through a series of events, it&nbsp;
causes a decrease. Working in reverse.

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:17.840
Ofcourse this bank angle causes&nbsp;
the Orbiter to drift off target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.840 --> 00:19:21.760
for its landing and to correct&nbsp;
it needs to bank the other way.

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:24.360
The orbiter has now slowed down to Mach 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:30.240
and at this point flight crew deploy air data&nbsp;
sensors to aid in the final approach. [Page 418]

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:34.640
Two probes rotate from underneath the&nbsp;
heat shield on either side of the nose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.640 --> 00:19:39.880
providing air speed, angle of attack and&nbsp;
temperature data to the flight computer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:44.920
At this point the orbiter is flying like a&nbsp;
plane, but it’s not a plane, it's a glider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:49.720
and the flight crew needed to carefully&nbsp;
manage the remaining energy to land safely.

00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:54.640
Especially as the orbiter is not built like&nbsp;
a traditional glider. Balancing the need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.640 --> 00:20:00.840
survive reentry, while maximising lift efficiency&nbsp;
was a unique engineering challenge, made more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:07.320
difficult by Air Force funding causing the design&nbsp;
to skew closer to a plane than a re-entry vehicle.

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:11.240
Early concepts of the shuttle&nbsp;
drew inspiration from the X-15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:16.920
with much smaller wings. More than enough to&nbsp;
land the original smaller orbiter on a runway.&nbsp;

00:20:16.920 --> 00:20:20.000
The air force wanted a larger payload capacity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:25.040
and they had one more specific requirement&nbsp;
that required bigger wings. They wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.040 --> 00:20:29.600
the Space Shuttle to be able to take off&nbsp;
from Cape Canaveral, complete its mission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:34.160
and land back at Cape Canaveral after&nbsp;
a single orbit. Essentially treating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:40.200
the space shuttle like a military operation&nbsp;
to avoid Soviet attention, fast and elusive

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:41.960
This came with some issues.

00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:47.080
A single orbit took 90 minutes, in which&nbsp;
time the earth rotated below the shuttle’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:52.760
orbit by 2000 kilometres. So, to return&nbsp;
to its launch location the orbiter would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.760 --> 00:20:58.240
need to be capable of flying laterally by&nbsp;
at least 2000 kilometres. This is called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.240 --> 00:21:06.400
crossrange. The original X-15 inspired design&nbsp;
was capable of just 370 kilometres of crossrange.

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:11.880
North American Rockwell proposed this huge&nbsp;
blended body delta wing concept in 1970&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:16.240
which would have given the orbiter 2800&nbsp;
kilometres of crossrange (1500 nautical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:20.400
miles). The full reusable concept,&nbsp;
with its crewed booster rocket and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:25.000
internal fuel tanks were not to be,&nbsp;
but the large delta wings endured.

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:30.360
These delta wings provided enough lift in both&nbsp;
hypersonic, supersonic and subsonic flight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:36.440
regimes to provide the cross range needed to reach&nbsp;
the original launch site after a single orbit.

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:41.280
Despite that, the space shuttle was an&nbsp;
unwieldy aircraft, and with no powered flight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:47.080
the commander had one shot to land it safely.&nbsp;
So they had to go through intensive training.

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:53.720
In 1973 NASA created 4 training aircraft by&nbsp;
modifying 4 gulfstream jets to fly like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:58.240
space shuttle. [REF] In order to mimic the&nbsp;
immense drag the blunt body design of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.240 --> 00:22:03.000
space shuttle would create, the gulfstreams flew&nbsp;
with their landing gear down, with their engines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:08.960
working in reverse, and flew with their flaps&nbsp;
deflected upwards to decrease lift. [Footage]

00:22:08.960 --> 00:22:13.160
With flight characteristics like this the&nbsp;
space shuttle needed to carefully manage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:18.520
its energy. Losing too much altitude far&nbsp;
from the runway would be far from ideal.

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:23.360
The pilots aim, guided by beacons and&nbsp;
ground control, for a tangential entry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.360 --> 00:22:29.360
into a circular approach path 5800 metres&nbsp;
in diameter just off the runway. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.360 --> 00:22:34.480
moment they intercept this circle the space&nbsp;
shuttle commences a deep spiralling turn.

00:22:34.480 --> 00:22:40.400
At this point the space shuttle is still&nbsp;
travelling at about 0.8 mach. Still very fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:46.280
and it's at this point the wings are stressed&nbsp;
the most. Hitting a higher max dynamic pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.280 --> 00:22:52.440
than at any point during the re-entry process&nbsp;
due the higher air pressure at this altitude.

00:22:52.440 --> 00:22:55.040
And the shuttle's descent rate increases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.040 --> 00:23:00.960
now losing altitude rapidly with the knowledge&nbsp;
the runway is within a safe gliding distance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:06.640
descending to about 3000 metres in&nbsp;
altitude and slowing to about 0.5 mach.

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:08.960
At this point the Shuttle enters a straight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:13.400
20 degree glide approach path.&nbsp;
Much steeper than any airliner.

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:18.680
Bleeding speed as needed using the&nbsp;
split rudder speed brake. The rudder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.680 --> 00:23:23.640
on the vertical tail wasn’t just used for&nbsp;
controlling yaw by deflecting left and right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:29.520
it could split in two, deflecting outwards&nbsp;
to a maximum of 62 degrees on either side.

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:33.840
The gear is lowered as late as possible&nbsp;
and in the final moments before touching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:37.680
down the commander will increase the&nbsp;
angle of attack of the shuttle using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:42.160
the elevons in order to slow its&nbsp;
descent rate before touching down.

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:45.920
At the moment of main gear touch down&nbsp;
the speed brake is commanded to fully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:51.360
open. At this point the shuttle is still&nbsp;
travelling at 360 kilometres per hour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:55.600
close to 0.3 mach, and there is&nbsp;
plenty of braking left to do.

00:23:55.600 --> 00:24:01.520
The runway at Cape Canaveral is 4.6 kilometres&nbsp;
long, much longer than your average runway. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.520 --> 00:24:07.000
typical 3 kilometres long runways being able to&nbsp;
deal with the heaviest of jumbo jets landing.

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:10.880
As the nose gear touches down the&nbsp;
electro-hydraulic brakes in each of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:17.600
the 4 main landing gear wheels are fully engaged&nbsp;
by application of the foot pedals in the cockpit.&nbsp;

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:21.240
Each brake assembly had nine&nbsp;
carbon-lined beryllium discs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:26.160
four rotors, and five stators, which were&nbsp;
pressed together to provide braking force.

00:24:26.160 --> 00:24:31.760
On STS 49, Bruce Melnicks mission, an&nbsp;
additional braking mechanism was introduced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:37.680
hidden away underneath the vertical tail speed&nbsp;
brake. The drag shoot. STS 49 was the maiden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:42.920
flight of both the endeavour and the drag&nbsp;
shoot. An explosively deployed parachute, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:48.160
can even see the parachute door and sabot being&nbsp;
flung from the back of the shuttle on landings.

00:24:48.160 --> 00:24:54.040
It was designed to allow the Space Shuttle&nbsp;
to land on a shorter 2500 metre runway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.040 --> 00:24:57.760
a contingency plan in the event&nbsp;
the space shuttle had to abort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.760 --> 00:25:02.040
a launch and land on a runway on&nbsp;
the other side of the atlantic.

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:07.000
For each and every launch NASA sent staff&nbsp;
to these predetermined landing locations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.520
like Fairford RAF base in Gloucestershire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:09.520 --> 00:25:15.520
England. A large team was needed to assist with&nbsp;
the final moments of each space shuttle mission.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:15.520 --> 00:25:20.640
And these teams were not once needed in the&nbsp;
entire history of the space shuttle program.

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:25.880
A giant fan was even rolled out on the runway&nbsp;
to help disperse any potential toxic chemicals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.880 --> 00:25:28.720
like the hydrazine fuel, away&nbsp;
from the shuttle. [FOOTAGE]

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:34.880
The mission is now over. The crew has disembarked&nbsp;
and the space shuttle will be refurbished and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:40.480
flown back to Florida on the back of a&nbsp;
747 in preparation for its next mission.

00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:46.040
People often say how incredible it is that&nbsp;
older aerospace and aviation projects like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:51.480
this were designed with pen and paper. Hand&nbsp;
drafted technical design was an art form,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:54.160
and a labour intensive art form at that.

00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:57.760
Today we computer aided design&nbsp;
makes things vastly easier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

